5a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) have the joint probability mass function ![](images/a2/a2dcac06a4cf671df0fa43cef1727449fb2ebc57fb824d19cc07da5e75d12176.svg) shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ![](images/73/73200164e7e4ed0fcd6d2b30f32e740f7a144fb66ee76bf8a22b3c39f958dfb3.svg) is defined as\n\n![](images/d0/d0b202fd64d7298dd7f1a4c4e80ae8ba1f1186ec4e90662ae45f73da799c253b.svg)\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n![](images/61/6195d748dc9306bdc6742a95785120c1db0875adc83fd07608bd023efd19be7a.svg)\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n![](images/20/2009dc5dd1ad1738cc1f50345734656e3970ab103d191929404b0ce7aa70da8f.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/a2/a2dcac06a4cf671df0fa43cef1727449fb2ebc57fb824d19cc07da5e75d12176.svg) is the joint probability mass function of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/07/07fd689b67975f50da1b6b025460996fc87f21f758f5e98d34eb84c9b0e50fcd.svg) and ![](images/3a/3aa9c47fc054317f2163393853c05d71714e0c9f0424297f51b46b107e222229.svg) is the marginal distribution of ![](images/02/029615e92d8b6d5c444a4daf74a9a8d63048e8d4be5f97c8207fe7af2e189e18.svg)\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) | ![](images/5d/5dc49d43a26a7f7c244230d64b45a8f4347c9c0c9459c2e84d09c77bab196a6a.svg) | ![](images/3b/3b30c77d4719f1a02bb918bac935a9700f7895d4a08995493dca6a9707a59888.svg) | ![](images/0d/0d08226a482630bff20558817aceb35b7ba82c3f1b1cfe5143deb07deb8726f8.svg) | ![](images/fd/fdb49440ceb7e11cd9ba0b0869a1e6d3565549969180830d1796db7dd00a2b96.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/8b/8b60f9aefd30405c3034eec74ec8331ef8082ea222b7770741d259cb873f0edb.svg) | ![](images/82/82f400b1c1ecaa2224d27d706d714b647de00fc52289bc0979e86421102f377a.svg) | ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) | ![](images/68/68dade17027ee6000e173cc4300792d43ded4611ea76ccd1bbbe944d0f1c33e4.svg) | ![](images/ae/ae6c6f895c67f3c99f98c0aa70dbcaaa6d553079bc8942a85b95d36458731a6b.svg) |\n| ![](images/74/74c80a223d4f01acbb32630508e2233df51abac691108b092579afecffa81eed.svg) | ![](images/ae/ae6c6f895c67f3c99f98c0aa70dbcaaa6d553079bc8942a85b95d36458731a6b.svg) | ![](images/82/82f400b1c1ecaa2224d27d706d714b647de00fc52289bc0979e86421102f377a.svg) | ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) | ![](images/43/43dec3bb0f167a8fc4d34f800d49bcc041c36b6da8c5df0ab6eff97d1804d5c6.svg) |\n| ![](images/2a/2af6da210d7b2077279622a81cd96521db7accd90fa91c8f1a467c7c0ea63ecc.svg) | ![](images/82/82f400b1c1ecaa2224d27d706d714b647de00fc52289bc0979e86421102f377a.svg) | ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) | ![](images/68/68dade17027ee6000e173cc4300792d43ded4611ea76ccd1bbbe944d0f1c33e4.svg) | ![](images/ae/ae6c6f895c67f3c99f98c0aa70dbcaaa6d553079bc8942a85b95d36458731a6b.svg) |\n| ![](images/3a/3aa9c47fc054317f2163393853c05d71714e0c9f0424297f51b46b107e222229.svg) | ![](images/0b/0b47cf9f83219ef6bab2ac82c70ded537ce9db2ab3945b85d5ca678f16b30a75.svg) | ![](images/ae/ae6c6f895c67f3c99f98c0aa70dbcaaa6d553079bc8942a85b95d36458731a6b.svg) | ![](images/68/68dade17027ee6000e173cc4300792d43ded4611ea76ccd1bbbe944d0f1c33e4.svg) | ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) |\n\nTherefore, for ![](images/02/027b707633da7c0c044a94992bbc3e3af329c5637a1d359c2a7dc8d7f87adf64.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/df/df523a1ccd89260e6b6d9d722b5339bf045050e30100c86c75dff65a75d192a6.svg)\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) | ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) | ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) | ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/39/393248bc977a944f226b63d6e427db4a75fae5d9bb8223b1dd7e20dc7c6c915d.svg) | ![](images/93/93e08787a61d8cca64eb3502ac419eb60504d75ad064927243c22a315430ab3e.svg) | ![](images/16/1627bb3010ada55ed941966d38081f1a6c1394c07ca78b94585f8c97e4bbc138.svg) | ![](images/93/93e08787a61d8cca64eb3502ac419eb60504d75ad064927243c22a315430ab3e.svg) |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161671 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "277b8807-fb74-5ad3-a89b-92dbf90bc075" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Conditional Probability Mass Function at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "0e42cf0f-f1bd-5762-a4fc-3cedec697c14" :question/math-academy-id "e-10050" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ |\n\nCompute $f_{X|Y}(2 \\,|\\, 2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function $f_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) = P(X = x \\,|\\, Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(2 \\,|\\, 2) = P(X = 2 \\,|\\, Y = 2).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X = 2 \\,|\\, Y = 2) = \\dfrac{P(X = 2, Y = 2)}{P(Y = 2)} = \\dfrac{f(2,2)}{f_Y(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(2) & =f(1,2)+f(2,2)+f(3,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(2,2) = \\dfrac{1}{6}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  $f_Y(2) = \\dfrac{7}{18}$ and $f(2,2) = \\dfrac{1}{6}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(2\\,|\\,2) & =P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=2) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{7}{18})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161876 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162076 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87675618-337f-5af2-8ad7-9132c080785a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "ddfe8dda-9ab2-5b11-af30-159a7bd32d64" :question/math-academy-id "e-9256" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.5$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ |\n\nCompute $P(Y=0 \\,|\\, X=1)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(Y = 0 \\,|\\, X = 1) = \\dfrac{P(X = 1 , Y = 0)}{P(X = 1)} =  \\dfrac{f(1,0)}{f_X(1)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_X(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(1) & =f(1,0)+f(1,1) \\\\  & =0.15+0.3 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(1,0) = 0.15.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $f_X(1) = 0.45$ and $f(1,0) = 0.15$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=1) & =\\frac{f(1,0)}{{f}_{X}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.45} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161877 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162077 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348dfcb8-2325-5929-b622-5423b7e37a53" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "531b9a19-e424-59e5-8c02-7bca4f67e110" :question/math-academy-id "e-9258" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCompute $P(0\\lt Y \\lt 3\\,|\\, X=0  )$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<Y<3\\,|\\,X=0) & =\\frac{P((0<Y<3),X=0)}{P(X=0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=0,(0<Y<3))}{P(X=0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=0,Y=1)+P(X=0,Y=2)}{P(X=0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(0,1)+f(0,2)}{{f}_{X}(0)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_X(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(0) & =f(0,0)+f(0,1)+f(0,2)+f(0,3) \\\\  & =0.2+0+0.05+0.1 \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(0,1)=0$ and $f(0,2)=0.05.$ \n\nFinally, substituting our values into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<Y<3\\,|\\,X=0) & =\\frac{f(0,1)+f(0,2)}{{f}_{X}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0.05}{0.35} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.05}{0.35} \\\\  & ≈0.14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162076 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87675618-337f-5af2-8ad7-9132c080785a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d076baa-16c1-5ead-8baf-b71b24793b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159435} {:answer/id #uuid "cee24829-5b33-5cbf-988a-e9eef36f783c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159436} {:answer/id #uuid "01a41e10-e5dc-5030-993c-64c9c2d1973a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159437} {:answer/id #uuid "4dba2cd5-fb6d-5a6c-a2b0-363cd95e9ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159438} {:answer/id #uuid "55369d5b-546b-5bbe-a91d-535fb8fb3367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cee24829-5b33-5cbf-988a-e9eef36f783c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159436} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c8215f3-4bea-5552-8b5e-4c1c809315eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a22ec54-0726-5bc9-a10c-e9f017685aa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142547" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(Y=3\\,|\\,X=2)=\\frac{P(X=2,Y=3)}{P(X=2)}=\\frac{f(2,3)}{{f}_{X}(2)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(2) & =0.05+0.25 \\\\  & =0.30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(2,3)=0.25.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{X}(2)=0.30$ and $f(2,3)=0.25$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,3)}{{f}_{X}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.25}{0.30} \\\\  & ≈0.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162104 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebabf742-2b8d-5392-a077-f34e8f4bd034" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Mass Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d64482-a157-593b-a0d1-db5b6744fac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427178} {:answer/id #uuid "33614170-1060-5986-98ed-e0d47798ec54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427182} {:answer/id #uuid "9439ac72-3a37-5eb2-990d-61250e8eb639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427191} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c0f42e-45c2-566f-9ec2-8be256c299d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427196} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdc667d-fd73-5bf1-b510-9e033c858f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9439ac72-3a37-5eb2-990d-61250e8eb639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427191} :answer-field/id #uuid "eef8539b-508b-5fa7-a6fb-1b79927d1667" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8cfbd164-6093-5409-a707-eefa23c3aa6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142554" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)=\\frac{P(X=2,Y=y)}{P(X=2)}=\\frac{f(2,y)}{{f}_{X}(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in \\{0,1,2\\},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,0)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ P(Y=1\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,1)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.3}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162077 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348dfcb8-2325-5929-b622-5423b7e37a53" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "558ce6b2-7ed9-580c-a0dc-2f742abaac0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427185} {:answer/id #uuid "5da297a0-8c40-5743-92dd-89674c59b084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427186} {:answer/id #uuid "778478e6-34f7-5241-9c21-a6104ba3033d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427188} {:answer/id #uuid "b8532e0e-af6a-5a33-b916-4185a356c949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427194} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc7ea99-dc34-5c5e-b665-c3586432a818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfc7ea99-dc34-5c5e-b665-c3586432a818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427199} :answer-field/id #uuid "28ba60db-712f-5d88-8bab-1c42ece049bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f05ade6-f4a6-518d-b188-3b387ae00a2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142862" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ |\n\nCompute $P(1<Y<7\\,|\\,X=2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<Y<7\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{P((1<Y<7),X=2)}{P(X=2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=2,(1<Y<7))}{P(X=2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=2,Y=3)+P(X=2,Y=5)}{P(X=2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2,3)+f(2,5)}{{f}_{X}(2)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(2) & =f(2,1)+f(2,3)+f(2,5)+f(2,7) \\\\  & =0+0.05+0.05+0.1 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(2,3)=0.05$ and $f(2,5)=0.05.$ \n\nFinally, substituting our values into our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<Y<7\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,3)+f(2,5)}{{f}_{X}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.05+0.05}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1}{0.2} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162076 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87675618-337f-5af2-8ad7-9132c080785a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7df7cc62-41f2-5b67-8003-b3f2f792fe42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427189} {:answer/id #uuid "9258e615-4af2-514e-a284-343d0ce33226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427190} {:answer/id #uuid "cece1cdf-fe31-5b7b-9e6c-984391d3198f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427198} {:answer/id #uuid "dd26a283-a544-5be7-b680-07b8f95e6c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427200} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b91a68-f1a7-5800-9852-2b8c8adc0a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9b91a68-f1a7-5800-9852-2b8c8adc0a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427201} :answer-field/id #uuid "267ccec5-8a84-5cdf-a568-00ac33d5a7c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd1f8e38-c35e-55be-bdf0-b84dac107dde" :question/math-academy-id "q-142884" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ |\n\nCompute $P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(Y=0\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=0)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,0)}{{f}_{X}(3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(3) & =f(3,0)+f(3,1)+f(3,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{18}+\\frac{2}{18}+\\frac{1}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(3,0)=\\frac{1}{9}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{X}(3)=\\frac{5}{18}$ and $f(3,0)=\\frac{1}{9}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,0)}{{f}_{X}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{9})}{(\\frac{5}{18})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "277b8807-fb74-5ad3-a89b-92dbf90bc075" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Conditional Probability Mass Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "772f1af4-3e1a-5d24-b6f2-84e08728280e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363282} {:answer/id #uuid "ed679624-c3a1-592b-b00b-85dbdf0ab4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363283} {:answer/id #uuid "65636e3f-fc1c-570a-afb9-b6e83ab76155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363284} {:answer/id #uuid "145ae614-6fb3-5df0-adc4-a7a23c2f414d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363285} {:answer/id #uuid "e6479b1c-edbb-5852-9f86-63ee058f8c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed679624-c3a1-592b-b00b-85dbdf0ab4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363283} :answer-field/id #uuid "945c9fdc-f2f4-578d-8093-3c96735abd64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e911b538-24df-51a8-a134-2c4a130edca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-142890" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,2)=P(X=4\\,|\\,Y=2).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=4\\,|\\,Y=2)=\\frac{P(X=4,Y=2)}{P(Y=2)}=\\frac{f(4,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(2) & =f(2,2)+f(4,2) \\\\  & =0.4+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(4,2)=0.15.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{Y}(2)=0.55$ and $f(4,2)=0.15$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,2) & =P(X=4\\,|\\,Y=2) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.55} \\\\  & ≈0.27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "277b8807-fb74-5ad3-a89b-92dbf90bc075" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Conditional Probability Mass Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b23a91dd-7a01-54db-9c82-1cde58ce273a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363289} {:answer/id #uuid "8145968b-18ea-5743-b1bd-efc55f689163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363290} {:answer/id #uuid "57859f6d-2835-5a6a-af22-856e77c2c686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363291} {:answer/id #uuid "771f4df6-0fd2-59bd-b1c4-8a07438c5de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363292} {:answer/id #uuid "7678ab30-3836-51cd-9e08-1245f5e719d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "771f4df6-0fd2-59bd-b1c4-8a07438c5de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363292} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c83bc5-8c9c-5ba3-9c2d-a22c540d169d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37840033-8bd0-59aa-a8ed-245c50ed8426" :question/math-academy-id "q-142892" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{9}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{X|Y}(1\\,|\\,4)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(1\\,|\\,4)=P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4)=\\frac{P(X=1,Y=4)}{P(Y=4)}=\\frac{f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(4) & =f(1,4)+f(3,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{20}+\\frac{1}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(1,4)=\\frac{7}{20}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{Y}(4)=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $f(1,4)=\\frac{7}{20}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(1\\,|\\,4) & =P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{20})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162077 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348dfcb8-2325-5929-b622-5423b7e37a53" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a5a9d4-3a44-5769-9f6b-52e2cb75f730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427180} {:answer/id #uuid "2f47c227-7ea9-5c71-8c0a-c370a8c2740e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427181} {:answer/id #uuid "4bcd313c-8a81-5a91-877f-2970fdc8ac87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427184} {:answer/id #uuid "bb32d8a6-9c26-5a27-9faa-26691c347856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427195} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a0b152-342f-53f4-afe1-60a1eb558763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a5a9d4-3a44-5769-9f6b-52e2cb75f730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427180} :answer-field/id #uuid "78621176-1e00-508c-804a-571ad3daf5d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29c9824b-2b87-54bb-a940-69ce3efe0d1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-142904" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nCompute $P(0\\le X<2\\,|\\,Y=4)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X<2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{P((0\\le X<2),Y=4)}{P(Y=4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=0,Y=4)+P(X=1,Y=4)}{P(Y=4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(0,4)+f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(4) & =f(0,4)+f(1,4)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.05+0.1+0.05 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(0,4)=0.05$ and $f(1,4)=0.1.$ \n\nFinally, substituting our values into our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X<2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(0,4)+f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.05+0.1}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.2} \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162104 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebabf742-2b8d-5392-a077-f34e8f4bd034" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Mass Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f6b37e-e1c4-56fe-af0d-d05220252493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427179} {:answer/id #uuid "4329cac3-a508-561d-92e3-75791b4b2a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427183} {:answer/id #uuid "6429f8f1-6f22-5c87-b7fa-2aa976d2cf28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427187} {:answer/id #uuid "b4efca0f-c1ba-5117-b9f7-7813f9a55b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427192} {:answer/id #uuid "b80e5bda-3113-58c2-972b-15fe4ed4a1f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4efca0f-c1ba-5117-b9f7-7813f9a55b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427192} :answer-field/id #uuid "295a6c8e-d0f1-5ddc-b043-2d9d231b86a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ab049c7-8771-5b28-bd3b-011a826d75ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-142936" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=2)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=x\\,|\\,Y=2)=\\frac{P(X=x,Y=2)}{P(Y=2)}=\\frac{f(x,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $x\\in \\{0,2,4\\},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(0,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.4}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ P(X=4\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(4,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.4}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |"}]}
